Protect the user's configuration files (config-file-protection)

Patch B gives an administrator settings that the user's processes cannot
change, but a user configuration still lives in files that any process
running as the user can rewrite, and a configuration can set the command
Ghostty runs. Keeping a user configuration safe from those processes means
loading only files the user cannot change: files owned by someone else, or
files that carry a flag only root can clear.

A new key, config-file-protection = required, does that. It is honored only
in the system file and captured right after the system file loads, like
config-default-files. The default is off, which behaves as upstream. With
required, a file loads only if the running user can neither change it nor
replace it. That covers every configuration file loaded after the system
file: the default files, a file named by GHOSTTY_CONFIG_PATH, every
config-file (nested ones too) and every theme file. It also covers every
file a setting names: custom-shader, background-image, bell-audio-path,
gtk-custom-css, input = path:..., and macos-custom-icon with macos-icon =
custom.

The check is patch B's walk from / with another rule for owners. An entry
owned by anyone but the user passes. An entry the user owns passes only
with a flag that only root can clear: schg, or sappnd on a directory
(macOS and FreeBSD). Flags the user can clear, such as uchg, never count,
and Linux has no such exception. The mode and ACL rules stay as in B,
except that a macOS ACL on a directory may allow adding entries, since
adding an entry cannot replace one that exists.

Configuration files are read through the descriptor the check opened. Files
a setting names are checked when the configuration is finalized and opened
by path later. That is safe, because a path that passes is pinned against
the user. A file that fails is not loaded, or its setting is dropped, and
the configuration-errors window says why. The rest of the configuration
still loads.

With required, file locations never come from the environment. The default
files and the user theme directory are found from the home directory in the
user database, not from HOME, XDG_CONFIG_HOME or CFFIXED_USER_HOME. No
template file is written, and an input path must be absolute. Once any
system file is loaded, ~/ in a path also expands from the user database,
in the system file itself and in every file after it.

